.TH NNGREP 1 "Release 6.3".UC 4.SH NAMEnngrep \- grep for news group names.SH SYNOPSIS.B nngrep[ \-\fBa\fP ].I pattern.SH DESCRIPTION.B nngrepscan \fInn\fP's rc file for news group names that matches the.I pattern and print their names on the standard output..LPFor example, to get the names of all "source" groups, you can use thecommand .br	nngrep source.LPNormally, \fInngrep\fP will only print names of groups to which you subscribe..LPIf you specify the \-a option, it will print all matching groups inthe rc file..LPYou can use this to read a specific subset of news groups with\fInn\fP; for example.br	nn -x -snn `nngrep source`.SH FILES.DT.ta \w'~/.nn/rc.bak'u+3m.\"ta 0 12~/.nn/rc	The record of read articles.DT.SH SEE ALSOnn(1), nncheck(1), nngoback(1), nngrep(1).brnnadmin(1M), nnquery(1M), nnusage(1M), nnmaster(8).SH BUGSThe rc file only contains entries for the groups you have actuallyread, i.e. new groups which you have not yet read are not printed.Therefore, groups in which no articles have ever been posted will notshow up either..LPThis is actually a feature of \fInn\fP:  it willdelay the entry of new groups into the rc file, so it can tell youthat it is a new group the first time you read it!.SH AUTHORKim F. Storm, Texas Instruments A/S, Denmark.brE-mail: storm@texas.dk